window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-09-2", "source": "2020年09月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the saying “What is worth doing is worth doing well ”, You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "The proverb \"What is worth doing is worth doing well\" carries a simple yet profound message: once we decide\nthat something is worth our effort, we should pursue excellence rather than settle for a careless, half-finished\njob. I firmly agree with this view.\nTo begin with, doing things well is the surest path to personal growth. When a student is devoted to a task and\nstrives for perfection, every detail he polishes sharpens his own ability. Take my roommate, who rewrote his\ngraduation thesis five times; the effort not only earned him an honour but also trained the patience he now\nrelies on at work.\nFurthermore, the spirit of doing well drives society forward. Great engineers and craftsmen who spare no effort\nto refine their products give us the reliable bridges, phones and medicines we depend on every day. Had they\nbeen content with a rough result, progress would slow and quality would collapse.\nIn conclusion, whatever is worth our time deserves our very best. As young students, we should abandon the\nget-it-done-quickly attitude and devote ourselves to every task, big or small, until it is truly done well."}, "translation": {"prompt": "《红楼梦》(Dream of the Red Chamber )是18世纪曹雪芹创作的一部小说。曹雪芹基于自己痛苦的个人经历，讲述了贾宝玉和林黛玉之间的悲剧性爱情故事。书中有大约30个主要人物和400多个次要人物，每个人物都刻画得栩栩如生，具有鲜明的个性。小说详尽地描述了四个贵族世家兴衰的历程，反映了封建社会隐藏的种种危机和错综复杂的社会冲突。 《红楼梦》融合了现实主义和浪漫主义，具有很强的艺术感染力。它被普遍认为是中国最伟大的小说，也是世界上最伟大的文学创作之一。", "sample": "Dream of the Red Chamber is a novel written by Cao Xueqin in the 18th century. Based on his own painful personal experience, Cao Xueqin told the tragic love story between Jia Baoyu and Lin Daiyu. There are about 30 major characters and more than 400 minor ones in the book, and each character is portrayed vividly, with a distinct personality. The novel describes in detail the rise and fall of four aristocratic families, and reflects the hidden crises and complicated social conflicts of feudal society. It combines realism and romanticism, and has great artistic appeal. It is widely regarded as the greatest novel in China and one of the greatest literary works in the world."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "It was perhaps when my parents—who also happen to be my housemates—left to go traveling for a couple of months recently that it  __26__  on me why I had not yet left the family home.\nIt wasn't that I relied on them for  __27__  reasons, or to keep my life in order, or to ease the chaos of the home. These days, I rely on them for their company.\nI missed coming home and talking about my day at work, and I missed being able to read their faces and sense how their day was. I missed having unique  __28__  into tiny details that make a life.\nWhile the conversation about young adults staying longer at home is  __29__  by talk of laziness, of dependence, of an inability for young people to pull themselves together,  __30__  do we talk of the way, in my case at least, my relationship with my parents has  __31__  strengthened the longer we have lived together.\nOver the years the power dynamic has changed and is no longer defined by one being the giver and another, the taker. So, what does this say for our relationships within the family home?\nAccording to psychologist Sabina Read, there are \"some very positive possible  __32__  when adult children share the family home\", noting the \"parent-child relationship may indeed strengthen and mature\" in the process.\nBut, she notes, a strong  __33__  doesn't simply come with time. \"The many changing factors of the relationship need to be acknowledged, rather than hoping that the mere passage of time will  __34__  connect parents to their adult children. It's important to acknowledge that the relationship parameters have changed to avoid falling back into  __35__  from the teen years.\"", "matchIntro": "How Telemedicine Is Transforming Healthcare", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "After years of big promises, telemedicine is finally living up to its potential. Driven by faster internet connections, ubiquitous(无处不在的) smartphones and changing insurance standards, more health providers are turning to electronic communications to do their jobs—and it's dramatically changing the delivery of healthcare."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Doctors are linking up with patients by phone, email and webcam(网络摄像头). They're also consulting with each other electronically—sometimes to make split-second decisions on heart attacks and strokes. Patients, meanwhile, are using new devices to relay their blood pressure, heart rate and other vital signs to their doctors so they can manage chronic conditions at home. Telemedicine also allows for better care in places where medical expertise is hard to come by. Five to 10 times a day, Doctors Without Borders relays questions about tough cases from its physicians in Niger, South Sudan and elsewhere to its network of 280 experts around the world, and back again via internet."}, {"label": "C", "text": "As a measure of how rapidly telemedicine is spreading, consider: More than 15 million Americans received some kind of medical care remotely last year, according to the American Telemedicine Association, a trade group, which expects those numbers to grow by 30% this year."}, {"label": "D", "text": "None of this is to say that telemedicine has found its way into all corners of medicine. A recent survey of 500 tech-savvy(精通技术的) consumers found that 39% hadn't heard of telemedicine, and of those who haven't used it, 42% said they preferred in-person doctor visits. In a poll of 1500 family physicians, only 15% had used it in their practices—but 90% said they would if it were appropriately reimbursed(补偿)."}, {"label": "E", "text": "What's more, for all the rapid growth, significant questions and challenges remain. Rules defining and regulating telemedicine differ widely from state to state. Physicians groups are issuing different guidelines about what care they consider appropriate to deliver and in what form."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Some critics also question whether the quality of care is keeping up with the rapid expansion of telemedicine. And there's the question of what services physicians should be paid for: Insurance coverage varies from health plan to health plan, and a big federal plan covers only a narrow range of services. Telemedicine's future will depend on how—and whether—regulators, providers, payers and patients can address these challenges. Here's a closer look at some of these issues:"}, {"label": "G", "text": "Do patients trade quality for convenience? The fastest-growing services in telemedicine connect consumers with clinicians they've never met for a phone, video or email visit—on-demand, 24/7. Typically, these are for nonemergency issues such as colds, flu, ear-aches and skin rashes, and they cost around $45, compared with approximately $100 at a doctor's office, $160 at an urgent-care clinic or $750 and up at an emergency room."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Many health plans and employers have rushed to offer the services and promote them as a convenient way for plan members to get medical care without leaving home or work. Nearly three-quarters of large employers will offer virtual doctor visits as a benefit to employees this year, up from 48% last year. Web companies such as Teladoc and American Well are expected to host some 1.2 million such virtual doctor visits this year, up 20% from last year, according to the American Telemedicine Association."}, {"label": "I", "text": "But critics worry that such services may be sacrificing quality for convenience. Consulting a random doctor patients will never meet, they say, further fragments the health-care system, and even minor issues such as upper respiratory(上呼吸道的) infections can't be thoroughly evaluated by a doctor who can't listen to your heart or feel your swollen glands. In a recent study, researchers posing as patients with skin problems sought help from 16 telemedicine sites—with unsettling results. In 62 encounters, fewer than one-third disclosed clinicians' credentials or let patients choose; only 32% discussed potential side effects of prescribed medications. Several sites misdiagnosed serious conditions, largely because they failed to ask basic follow-up questions, the researchers said. \"Telemedicine holds enormous promise, but these sites are just not ready for prime time,\" says Jack Resneck, the study's lead author."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The American Telemedicine Association and other organizations have started accreditation(鉴定) programs to identify top-quality telemedicine sites. The American Medical Association this month approved new ethical guidelines for telemedicine, calling for participating doctors to recognize the limitations of such services and ensure that they have sufficient information to make clinical recommendations."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Who pays for the services? While employers and health plans have been eager to cover virtual urgent-care visits, insurers have been far less willing to pay for telemedicine when doctors use phone, email or video to consult with existing patients about continuing issues. \"It's very hard to get paid unless you physically see the patient,\" says Peter Rasmussen, medical director of distance health at the Cleveland Clinic. Some 32 states have passed \"parity\"(等同的) laws requiring private insurers to reimburse doctors for services delivered remotely if the same service would be covered in person, though not necessarily at the same rate or frequency. Medicare lags further behind. The federal health plan for the elderly covers a small number of telemedicine services—only for beneficiaries in rural areas and only when the services are received in a hospital, doctor's office or clinic."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Bills to expand Medicare coverage of telemedicine have bipartisan(两党的) support in Congress. Opponents worry that such expansion would be costly for taxpayers, but advocates say it would save money in the long run."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Experts say more hospitals are likely to invest in telemedicine systems as they move away from fee-for-service payments and into managed-care-type contracts that give them a set fee to provide care for patients and allow them to keep any savings they achieve."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Is the state-by-state regulatory system outdated? Historically, regulation of medicine has been left to individual states. But some industry members contend that having 50 different sets of rules, licensing fees and even definitions of \"medical practice\" makes less sense in the era of telemedicine and is hampering its growth. Currently, doctors must have a valid license in the state where the patient is located to provide medical care, which means virtual-visit companies can match users only with locally licensed clinicians. It also causes administrative hassles(麻烦) for world-class medical centers that attract patients from across the country. At the Mayo Clinic, doctors who treat out-of-state patients can follow up with them via phone, email or web chats when they return home, but they can only discuss the conditions they treated in person. \"If the patient wants to talk about a new problem, the doctor has to be licensed in that state to discuss it. If not, the patient should talk to his primary-care physician about it,\" says Steve Ommen, who runs Mayo's Connected Care program."}, {"label": "O", "text": "To date, 17 states have joined a compact that will allow a doctor licensed in one member state to quickly obtain a license in another. While welcoming the move, some telemedicine advocates would prefer states to automatically honor one another's licenses, as they do with drivers' licenses. But states aren't likely to surrender control of medical practice, and most are considering new regulations. This year, more than 200 telemedicine-related bills have been introduced in 42 states, many regarding what services Medicaid will cover and whether payers should reimburse for remote patient monitoring. \"A lot of states are still trying to define telemedicine,\" says Lisa Robbin, chief advocacy officer for the Federation of State Medical Boards."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Danielle Steel, the 71-year-old romance novelist is notoriously productive, having published 179 books at a rate of up to seven a year. But a passing reference in a recent profile by Glamour magazine to her 20-hour workdays prompted an outpouring of admiration.\nSteel has given that 20-hour figure when describing her \"exhausting\" process in the past: \"I start the book and don't leave my desk until the first draft is finished.\" She goes from bed, to desk, to bath, to bed, avoiding all contact aside from phone calls with her nine children. \"I don't comb my hair for weeks,\" she says. Meals are brought to her desk, where she types until her fingers swell and her nails bleed.\nThe business news website Quartz held Steel up as an inspiration, writing that if only we all followed her \"actually extremely liberating\" example of industrious sleeplessness, we would be quick to see results.\nWell, indeed. With research results showing the cumulative effects of sleep loss and its impact on productivity, doubt has been voiced about the accuracy of Steel's self-assessment. Her output may be undeniable, but sceptics have suggested that she is guilty of erasing the role of ghost writers(代笔人) at worst, gross exaggeration at best.\nSteel says working 20 hours a day is \"pretty brutal physically.\" But is it even possible? \"No,\" says Maryanne Taylor of the Sleep Works. While you could work that long, the impact on productivity would make it hardly worthwhile. If Steel was routinely sleeping for four hours a night, she would be drastically underestimating the negative impact, says Alison Gardiner, founder of the sleep improvement programme Sleep station. \"It's akin to being drunk.\"\nIt's possible that Steel is exaggerating the demands of her schedule. Self-imposed sleeplessness has \"become a bit of a status symbol\", says Taylor, a misguided measure to prove how powerful and productive you are. Margaret Thatcher was also said to get by on four hours a night, while the 130-hour workweeks endured by tech heads have been held up as key to their success.\nThat is starting to change with increased awareness of the importance of sleep for mental health. \"People are starting to realise that sleep should not be something that you fit in between everything else,\" says Taylor.\nBut it is possible—if statistically extremely unlikely—that Steel could be born a \"short sleeper\" with an unusual body clock, says sleep expert Dr. Sophie Bostock. \"It's probably present in fewer than 1% of the population.\"\nEven if Steel does happen to be among that tiny minority, says Bostock, it's \"pretty irresponsible\" to suggest that 20-hour days are simply a question of discipline for the rest of us.", "47": "Danielle Steel, the 71-year-old romance novelist is notoriously productive, having published 179 books at a rate of up to seven a year. But a passing reference in a recent profile by Glamour magazine to her 20-hour workdays prompted an outpouring of admiration.\nSteel has given that 20-hour figure when describing her \"exhausting\" process in the past: \"I start the book and don't leave my desk until the first draft is finished.\" She goes from bed, to desk, to bath, to bed, avoiding all contact aside from phone calls with her nine children. \"I don't comb my hair for weeks,\" she says. Meals are brought to her desk, where she types until her fingers swell and her nails bleed.\nThe business news website Quartz held Steel up as an inspiration, writing that if only we all followed her \"actually extremely liberating\" example of industrious sleeplessness, we would be quick to see results.\nWell, indeed. With research results showing the cumulative effects of sleep loss and its impact on productivity, doubt has been voiced about the accuracy of Steel's self-assessment. Her output may be undeniable, but sceptics have suggested that she is guilty of erasing the role of ghost writers(代笔人) at worst, gross exaggeration at best.\nSteel says working 20 hours a day is \"pretty brutal physically.\" But is it even possible? \"No,\" says Maryanne Taylor of the Sleep Works. While you could work that long, the impact on productivity would make it hardly worthwhile. If Steel was routinely sleeping for four hours a night, she would be drastically underestimating the negative impact, says Alison Gardiner, founder of the sleep improvement programme Sleep station. \"It's akin to being drunk.\"\nIt's possible that Steel is exaggerating the demands of her schedule. Self-imposed sleeplessness has \"become a bit of a status symbol\", says Taylor, a misguided measure to prove how powerful and productive you are. Margaret Thatcher was also said to get by on four hours a night, while the 130-hour workweeks endured by tech heads have been held up as key to their success.\nThat is starting to change with increased awareness of the importance of sleep for mental health. \"People are starting to realise that sleep should not be something that you fit in between everything else,\" says Taylor.\nBut it is possible—if statistically extremely unlikely—that Steel could be born a \"short sleeper\" with an unusual body clock, says sleep expert Dr. Sophie Bostock. \"It's probably present in fewer than 1% of the population.\"\nEven if Steel does happen to be among that tiny minority, says Bostock, it's \"pretty irresponsible\" to suggest that 20-hour days are simply a question of discipline for the rest of us.", "48": "Danielle Steel, the 71-year-old romance novelist is notoriously productive, having published 179 books at a rate of up to seven a year. But a passing reference in a recent profile by Glamour magazine to her 20-hour workdays prompted an outpouring of admiration.\nSteel has given that 20-hour figure when describing her \"exhausting\" process in the past: \"I start the book and don't leave my desk until the first draft is finished.\" She goes from bed, to desk, to bath, to bed, avoiding all contact aside from phone calls with her nine children. \"I don't comb my hair for weeks,\" she says. Meals are brought to her desk, where she types until her fingers swell and her nails bleed.\nThe business news website Quartz held Steel up as an inspiration, writing that if only we all followed her \"actually extremely liberating\" example of industrious sleeplessness, we would be quick to see results.\nWell, indeed. With research results showing the cumulative effects of sleep loss and its impact on productivity, doubt has been voiced about the accuracy of Steel's self-assessment. Her output may be undeniable, but sceptics have suggested that she is guilty of erasing the role of ghost writers(代笔人) at worst, gross exaggeration at best.\nSteel says working 20 hours a day is \"pretty brutal physically.\" But is it even possible? \"No,\" says Maryanne Taylor of the Sleep Works. While you could work that long, the impact on productivity would make it hardly worthwhile. If Steel was routinely sleeping for four hours a night, she would be drastically underestimating the negative impact, says Alison Gardiner, founder of the sleep improvement programme Sleep station. \"It's akin to being drunk.\"\nIt's possible that Steel is exaggerating the demands of her schedule. Self-imposed sleeplessness has \"become a bit of a status symbol\", says Taylor, a misguided measure to prove how powerful and productive you are. Margaret Thatcher was also said to get by on four hours a night, while the 130-hour workweeks endured by tech heads have been held up as key to their success.\nThat is starting to change with increased awareness of the importance of sleep for mental health. \"People are starting to realise that sleep should not be something that you fit in between everything else,\" says Taylor.\nBut it is possible—if statistically extremely unlikely—that Steel could be born a \"short sleeper\" with an unusual body clock, says sleep expert Dr. Sophie Bostock. \"It's probably present in fewer than 1% of the population.\"\nEven if Steel does happen to be among that tiny minority, says Bostock, it's \"pretty irresponsible\" to suggest that 20-hour days are simply a question of discipline for the rest of us.", "49": "Danielle Steel, the 71-year-old romance novelist is notoriously productive, having published 179 books at a rate of up to seven a year. But a passing reference in a recent profile by Glamour magazine to her 20-hour workdays prompted an outpouring of admiration.\nSteel has given that 20-hour figure when describing her \"exhausting\" process in the past: \"I start the book and don't leave my desk until the first draft is finished.\" She goes from bed, to desk, to bath, to bed, avoiding all contact aside from phone calls with her nine children. \"I don't comb my hair for weeks,\" she says. Meals are brought to her desk, where she types until her fingers swell and her nails bleed.\nThe business news website Quartz held Steel up as an inspiration, writing that if only we all followed her \"actually extremely liberating\" example of industrious sleeplessness, we would be quick to see results.\nWell, indeed. With research results showing the cumulative effects of sleep loss and its impact on productivity, doubt has been voiced about the accuracy of Steel's self-assessment. Her output may be undeniable, but sceptics have suggested that she is guilty of erasing the role of ghost writers(代笔人) at worst, gross exaggeration at best.\nSteel says working 20 hours a day is \"pretty brutal physically.\" But is it even possible? \"No,\" says Maryanne Taylor of the Sleep Works. While you could work that long, the impact on productivity would make it hardly worthwhile. If Steel was routinely sleeping for four hours a night, she would be drastically underestimating the negative impact, says Alison Gardiner, founder of the sleep improvement programme Sleep station. \"It's akin to being drunk.\"\nIt's possible that Steel is exaggerating the demands of her schedule. Self-imposed sleeplessness has \"become a bit of a status symbol\", says Taylor, a misguided measure to prove how powerful and productive you are. Margaret Thatcher was also said to get by on four hours a night, while the 130-hour workweeks endured by tech heads have been held up as key to their success.\nThat is starting to change with increased awareness of the importance of sleep for mental health. \"People are starting to realise that sleep should not be something that you fit in between everything else,\" says Taylor.\nBut it is possible—if statistically extremely unlikely—that Steel could be born a \"short sleeper\" with an unusual body clock, says sleep expert Dr. Sophie Bostock. \"It's probably present in fewer than 1% of the population.\"\nEven if Steel does happen to be among that tiny minority, says Bostock, it's \"pretty irresponsible\" to suggest that 20-hour days are simply a question of discipline for the rest of us.", "50": "Danielle Steel, the 71-year-old romance novelist is notoriously productive, having published 179 books at a rate of up to seven a year. But a passing reference in a recent profile by Glamour magazine to her 20-hour workdays prompted an outpouring of admiration.\nSteel has given that 20-hour figure when describing her \"exhausting\" process in the past: \"I start the book and don't leave my desk until the first draft is finished.\" She goes from bed, to desk, to bath, to bed, avoiding all contact aside from phone calls with her nine children. \"I don't comb my hair for weeks,\" she says. Meals are brought to her desk, where she types until her fingers swell and her nails bleed.\nThe business news website Quartz held Steel up as an inspiration, writing that if only we all followed her \"actually extremely liberating\" example of industrious sleeplessness, we would be quick to see results.\nWell, indeed. With research results showing the cumulative effects of sleep loss and its impact on productivity, doubt has been voiced about the accuracy of Steel's self-assessment. Her output may be undeniable, but sceptics have suggested that she is guilty of erasing the role of ghost writers(代笔人) at worst, gross exaggeration at best.\nSteel says working 20 hours a day is \"pretty brutal physically.\" But is it even possible? \"No,\" says Maryanne Taylor of the Sleep Works. While you could work that long, the impact on productivity would make it hardly worthwhile. If Steel was routinely sleeping for four hours a night, she would be drastically underestimating the negative impact, says Alison Gardiner, founder of the sleep improvement programme Sleep station. \"It's akin to being drunk.\"\nIt's possible that Steel is exaggerating the demands of her schedule. Self-imposed sleeplessness has \"become a bit of a status symbol\", says Taylor, a misguided measure to prove how powerful and productive you are. Margaret Thatcher was also said to get by on four hours a night, while the 130-hour workweeks endured by tech heads have been held up as key to their success.\nThat is starting to change with increased awareness of the importance of sleep for mental health. \"People are starting to realise that sleep should not be something that you fit in between everything else,\" says Taylor.\nBut it is possible—if statistically extremely unlikely—that Steel could be born a \"short sleeper\" with an unusual body clock, says sleep expert Dr. Sophie Bostock. \"It's probably present in fewer than 1% of the population.\"\nEven if Steel does happen to be among that tiny minority, says Bostock, it's \"pretty irresponsible\" to suggest that 20-hour days are simply a question of discipline for the rest of us.", "51": "Organic agriculture is a relatively untapped resource for feeding the Earth's population, especially in the face of climate change and other global challenges. That's the conclusion I reached in reviewing 40 years of science comparing the long-term prospects of organic and conventional farming.\nThe review study, \"Organic Agriculture in the 21st Century,\" is featured as the cover story for the February issue of the journal Nature Plants. It is the first to compare organic and conventional agriculture across the main goals of sustainability identified by the National Academy of Sciences: productivity, economics, and environment.\nCritics have long argued that organic agriculture is inefficient, requiring more land to yield the same amount of food. It's true that organic farming produces lower yields, averaging 10 to 20 percent less than conventional. Advocates contend that the environmental advantages of organic agriculture far outweigh the lower yields, and that increasing research and breeding resources for organic systems would reduce the yield gap. Sometimes excluded from these arguments is the fact that we already produce enough food to more than feed the world's 7.4 billion people but do not provide adequate access to all individuals.\nIn some cases, organic yields can be higher than conventional. For example, in severe drought conditions, which are expected to increase with climate change in many areas, organic farms can produce as good, if not better, yields because of the higher water-holding capacity of organically farmed soils.\nWhat science does tell us is that mainstream conventional farming systems have provided growing supplies of food and other products but often at the expense of other sustainability goals.\nConventional agriculture may produce more food, but it often comes at a cost to the environment. Biodiversity loss, environmental degradation, and severe impacts on ecosystem services have not only accompanied conventional farming systems but have often extended well beyond their field boundaries. With organic agriculture, environmental costs tend to be lower and the benefits greater.\nOverall, organic farms tend to store more soil carbon, have better soil quality, and reduce soil erosion compared to their conventional counterparts. Organic agriculture also creates less soil and water pollution and lower greenhouse gas emissions. And it's more energy-efficient because it doesn't rely on synthetic fertilizers or pesticides.\nOrganic agriculture is also associated with greater biodiversity of plants, animals, insects and microorganisms as well as genetic diversity. Biodiversity increases the services that nature provides and improves the ability of farming systems to adapt to changing conditions.\nDespite lower yields, organic agriculture is more profitable for farmers because consumers are willing to pay more. Higher prices, called price premiums, can be justified as a way to compensate farmers for providing ecosystem services and avoiding environmental damage or external costs.", "52": "Organic agriculture is a relatively untapped resource for feeding the Earth's population, especially in the face of climate change and other global challenges. That's the conclusion I reached in reviewing 40 years of science comparing the long-term prospects of organic and conventional farming.\nThe review study, \"Organic Agriculture in the 21st Century,\" is featured as the cover story for the February issue of the journal Nature Plants. It is the first to compare organic and conventional agriculture across the main goals of sustainability identified by the National Academy of Sciences: productivity, economics, and environment.\nCritics have long argued that organic agriculture is inefficient, requiring more land to yield the same amount of food. It's true that organic farming produces lower yields, averaging 10 to 20 percent less than conventional. Advocates contend that the environmental advantages of organic agriculture far outweigh the lower yields, and that increasing research and breeding resources for organic systems would reduce the yield gap. Sometimes excluded from these arguments is the fact that we already produce enough food to more than feed the world's 7.4 billion people but do not provide adequate access to all individuals.\nIn some cases, organic yields can be higher than conventional. For example, in severe drought conditions, which are expected to increase with climate change in many areas, organic farms can produce as good, if not better, yields because of the higher water-holding capacity of organically farmed soils.\nWhat science does tell us is that mainstream conventional farming systems have provided growing supplies of food and other products but often at the expense of other sustainability goals.\nConventional agriculture may produce more food, but it often comes at a cost to the environment. Biodiversity loss, environmental degradation, and severe impacts on ecosystem services have not only accompanied conventional farming systems but have often extended well beyond their field boundaries. With organic agriculture, environmental costs tend to be lower and the benefits greater.\nOverall, organic farms tend to store more soil carbon, have better soil quality, and reduce soil erosion compared to their conventional counterparts. Organic agriculture also creates less soil and water pollution and lower greenhouse gas emissions. And it's more energy-efficient because it doesn't rely on synthetic fertilizers or pesticides.\nOrganic agriculture is also associated with greater biodiversity of plants, animals, insects and microorganisms as well as genetic diversity. Biodiversity increases the services that nature provides and improves the ability of farming systems to adapt to changing conditions.\nDespite lower yields, organic agriculture is more profitable for farmers because consumers are willing to pay more. Higher prices, called price premiums, can be justified as a way to compensate farmers for providing ecosystem services and avoiding environmental damage or external costs.", "53": "Organic agriculture is a relatively untapped resource for feeding the Earth's population, especially in the face of climate change and other global challenges. That's the conclusion I reached in reviewing 40 years of science comparing the long-term prospects of organic and conventional farming.\nThe review study, \"Organic Agriculture in the 21st Century,\" is featured as the cover story for the February issue of the journal Nature Plants. It is the first to compare organic and conventional agriculture across the main goals of sustainability identified by the National Academy of Sciences: productivity, economics, and environment.\nCritics have long argued that organic agriculture is inefficient, requiring more land to yield the same amount of food. It's true that organic farming produces lower yields, averaging 10 to 20 percent less than conventional. Advocates contend that the environmental advantages of organic agriculture far outweigh the lower yields, and that increasing research and breeding resources for organic systems would reduce the yield gap. Sometimes excluded from these arguments is the fact that we already produce enough food to more than feed the world's 7.4 billion people but do not provide adequate access to all individuals.\nIn some cases, organic yields can be higher than conventional. For example, in severe drought conditions, which are expected to increase with climate change in many areas, organic farms can produce as good, if not better, yields because of the higher water-holding capacity of organically farmed soils.\nWhat science does tell us is that mainstream conventional farming systems have provided growing supplies of food and other products but often at the expense of other sustainability goals.\nConventional agriculture may produce more food, but it often comes at a cost to the environment. Biodiversity loss, environmental degradation, and severe impacts on ecosystem services have not only accompanied conventional farming systems but have often extended well beyond their field boundaries. With organic agriculture, environmental costs tend to be lower and the benefits greater.\nOverall, organic farms tend to store more soil carbon, have better soil quality, and reduce soil erosion compared to their conventional counterparts. Organic agriculture also creates less soil and water pollution and lower greenhouse gas emissions. And it's more energy-efficient because it doesn't rely on synthetic fertilizers or pesticides.\nOrganic agriculture is also associated with greater biodiversity of plants, animals, insects and microorganisms as well as genetic diversity. Biodiversity increases the services that nature provides and improves the ability of farming systems to adapt to changing conditions.\nDespite lower yields, organic agriculture is more profitable for farmers because consumers are willing to pay more. Higher prices, called price premiums, can be justified as a way to compensate farmers for providing ecosystem services and avoiding environmental damage or external costs.", "54": "Organic agriculture is a relatively untapped resource for feeding the Earth's population, especially in the face of climate change and other global challenges. That's the conclusion I reached in reviewing 40 years of science comparing the long-term prospects of organic and conventional farming.\nThe review study, \"Organic Agriculture in the 21st Century,\" is featured as the cover story for the February issue of the journal Nature Plants. It is the first to compare organic and conventional agriculture across the main goals of sustainability identified by the National Academy of Sciences: productivity, economics, and environment.\nCritics have long argued that organic agriculture is inefficient, requiring more land to yield the same amount of food. It's true that organic farming produces lower yields, averaging 10 to 20 percent less than conventional. Advocates contend that the environmental advantages of organic agriculture far outweigh the lower yields, and that increasing research and breeding resources for organic systems would reduce the yield gap. Sometimes excluded from these arguments is the fact that we already produce enough food to more than feed the world's 7.4 billion people but do not provide adequate access to all individuals.\nIn some cases, organic yields can be higher than conventional. For example, in severe drought conditions, which are expected to increase with climate change in many areas, organic farms can produce as good, if not better, yields because of the higher water-holding capacity of organically farmed soils.\nWhat science does tell us is that mainstream conventional farming systems have provided growing supplies of food and other products but often at the expense of other sustainability goals.\nConventional agriculture may produce more food, but it often comes at a cost to the environment. Biodiversity loss, environmental degradation, and severe impacts on ecosystem services have not only accompanied conventional farming systems but have often extended well beyond their field boundaries. With organic agriculture, environmental costs tend to be lower and the benefits greater.\nOverall, organic farms tend to store more soil carbon, have better soil quality, and reduce soil erosion compared to their conventional counterparts. Organic agriculture also creates less soil and water pollution and lower greenhouse gas emissions. And it's more energy-efficient because it doesn't rely on synthetic fertilizers or pesticides.\nOrganic agriculture is also associated with greater biodiversity of plants, animals, insects and microorganisms as well as genetic diversity. Biodiversity increases the services that nature provides and improves the ability of farming systems to adapt to changing conditions.\nDespite lower yields, organic agriculture is more profitable for farmers because consumers are willing to pay more. Higher prices, called price premiums, can be justified as a way to compensate farmers for providing ecosystem services and avoiding environmental damage or external costs.", "55": "Organic agriculture is a relatively untapped resource for feeding the Earth's population, especially in the face of climate change and other global challenges. That's the conclusion I reached in reviewing 40 years of science comparing the long-term prospects of organic and conventional farming.\nThe review study, \"Organic Agriculture in the 21st Century,\" is featured as the cover story for the February issue of the journal Nature Plants. It is the first to compare organic and conventional agriculture across the main goals of sustainability identified by the National Academy of Sciences: productivity, economics, and environment.\nCritics have long argued that organic agriculture is inefficient, requiring more land to yield the same amount of food. It's true that organic farming produces lower yields, averaging 10 to 20 percent less than conventional. Advocates contend that the environmental advantages of organic agriculture far outweigh the lower yields, and that increasing research and breeding resources for organic systems would reduce the yield gap. Sometimes excluded from these arguments is the fact that we already produce enough food to more than feed the world's 7.4 billion people but do not provide adequate access to all individuals.\nIn some cases, organic yields can be higher than conventional. For example, in severe drought conditions, which are expected to increase with climate change in many areas, organic farms can produce as good, if not better, yields because of the higher water-holding capacity of organically farmed soils.\nWhat science does tell us is that mainstream conventional farming systems have provided growing supplies of food and other products but often at the expense of other sustainability goals.\nConventional agriculture may produce more food, but it often comes at a cost to the environment. Biodiversity loss, environmental degradation, and severe impacts on ecosystem services have not only accompanied conventional farming systems but have often extended well beyond their field boundaries. With organic agriculture, environmental costs tend to be lower and the benefits greater.\nOverall, organic farms tend to store more soil carbon, have better soil quality, and reduce soil erosion compared to their conventional counterparts. Organic agriculture also creates less soil and water pollution and lower greenhouse gas emissions. And it's more energy-efficient because it doesn't rely on synthetic fertilizers or pesticides.\nOrganic agriculture is also associated with greater biodiversity of plants, animals, insects and microorganisms as well as genetic diversity. Biodiversity increases the services that nature provides and improves the ability of farming systems to adapt to changing conditions.\nDespite lower yields, organic agriculture is more profitable for farmers because consumers are willing to pay more. Higher prices, called price premiums, can be justified as a way to compensate farmers for providing ecosystem services and avoiding environmental damage or external costs."}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-09-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) dawned\n动词\n【词性槽】It 形式主语 + 谓语动词位\n【依据】固定搭配 it dawned on sb that/why…\n\"it dawned on someone (that)…\" 是英语高频固定句型，意为\"某人忽然明白……\"；与 it\n__(26)__ on me why I had not yet left the family home 中 \"on me + why 从句\" 完全吻\n合，且与下文\"作者由父母离家方意识到自己迟迟未走的原因\"语义贴合。\n【竞争词】B) contemplated 搭配不成立 + 主语错位\ncontemplate 意为\"沉思\"，正确用法是\"某人 contemplate something\"，而非 \"it\ncontemplated on me\"；本句主语是 it，套不进 contemplate 的搭配框架。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— B) contemplated 与 C) dawned 都是\"想类\"过去分词形\n态。考生若不熟 it dawned on sb 句型，仅凭\"沉思\"语感就可能选 contemplated；但\ncontemplate 不可接 it 形主、不与 on me 搭配。防坑：记牢顿悟系句型 it\ndawned/occurred/struck + on sb。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·It 形式主语 + 谓语动词位"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) logistical\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 reasons 前缺定语\n【依据】与下句\"维持生活秩序、缓解家中混乱\"语义对位\nor to keep my life in order, or to ease the chaos of the home 列举的正是日常起居、家\n务打理这类\"后勤\"性事务，与 logistical（后勤的）精准呼应；作者要否认的就是这种\"靠父\n母管后勤\"的依赖。\n【竞争词】F) legislative 义场不符（立法的）\nlegislative 属政治法律语域，与家庭日常毫无交集，放进 It wasn't that I relied on them for\n__(27)__ reasons 完全跑题。\nM) saturated 搭配不成立\nsaturated reasons（饱和的理由）不构成英语惯用搭配，且 saturated 后面通常配市场、颜色\n或脂肪等具体名词，与抽象 reasons 不搭。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 reasons 前缺定语"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) insight\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 unique 后 + 介词 into 前 缺名词中心词\n【依据】固定搭配 insight into sth\ninsight into 表\"对……的洞察/深入了解\"，是英语高频搭配；I missed having unique\n__(28)__ into tiny details that make a life 表达\"我怀念对生活细节的独特洞察\"，意思与上\n文\"读懂父母脸色、感知他们一天\"环环相扣。\n【竞争词】A) bond 搭配指向不对（bond with，不是 bond into）\nbond 接的介词是 with/between，表示与某人的纽带；放进\"bond into tiny details\"既不通顺\n也无该搭配。\nD) hierarchy 语义跑偏\nhierarchy 表\"等级\"，与\"对生活小细节的独特了解\"语义完全无关，且本段语境是个人观察、不\n是组织结构。\nJ) outcomes 搭配方向不对（outcomes of，不是 outcomes into）\noutcomes 表\"结果\"，常与 of 搭配，且\"对细节的结果\"语义说不通。\nK) patterns 搭配错位\npatterns 多与 of/in 搭配，且\"独特的模式 into 细节\"表意混乱。\nN) stereotypes 感情色彩冲突\nstereotypes 带贬义\"刻板印象\"，与 unique（独特）形容\"对细节的体察\"矛盾，且无 into\n搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 unique 后 + 介词 in"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) saturated\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 后缺过去分词构成被动\n【依据】be saturated by/with 表\"被……充斥\"\nthe conversation … is __(29)__ by talk of laziness, of dependence, of an inability for\nyoung people to pull themselves together 紧跟三个并列的负面话题，正是\"对话被这些\n论调充斥/淹没\"的语境，saturated 在此为固定搭配。\n【竞争词】B) contemplated 搭配错位（沉思的对象不是 by 而是直接宾语）\ncontemplate 意为\"沉思、考虑\"，其被动形式后续接的是\"被某人思考\"，与 by talk of laziness\n这种\"被某种言论充斥\"的语义不匹配；且 conversation is contemplated 也不符合本段在描写\n舆论氛围的语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词 is 后缺过去分词构成被动"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) rarely\n副词\n【词性槽】句首倒装提示否定副词\n【依据】唯一能触发倒装的否定副词，且语义对照成立\n上文 the conversation … is __(29)__ by talk of laziness, of dependence 讲舆论充斥负面\n论调，本句用 rarely 形成转折——\"却很少有人谈到关系反而变强\"，逻辑链条 \"舆论铺天盖\n地的是 A，但我们很少谈 B\" 顺滑自然。\n【竞争词】I) magically 无否定色彩，不能触发倒装\nmagically 表\"魔术般地\"，不属否定/半否定副词，置于句首也不能触发 do 倒装；且语义上\"魔\n术般地我们谈到\"无解释力。\nO) undoubtedly 肯定语气，方向相反\nundoubtedly 表\"无疑地\"，是强肯定副词，无法引发倒装，且与下文要表达的\"却很少有人谈\n到\"逻辑方向完全相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·句首倒装提示否定副词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) undoubtedly\n副词\n【词性槽】助动词 has 与过去分词 strengthened 之间缺状语\n【依据】强肯定副词，与作者旗帜鲜明的立场吻合\n作者全文都在为\"成年同住可让亲子关系更紧密\"正名，本句更是亮明个人证词；my\nrelationship with my parents has __(31)__ strengthened the longer we have lived\ntogether 用 undoubtedly\"毫无疑问地\"强化\"关系确实变得更牢\"的肯定语气，与 Over the\nyears the power dynamic has changed 共同支撑论点。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·助动词 has 与过去分词 streng"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) outcomes\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 positive possible 后缺名词中心词\n【依据】positive outcomes 是固定搭配，且符合心理学家陈述的\"积极结果\"语境\nAccording to psychologist Sabina Read, there are \"some very positive possible\n__(32)__ when adult children share the family home\"：心理学家在描述\"成年子女同住\n可能带来的积极结果\"，positive outcomes（积极的结果/成效）是英语里最高频的搭配\n之一。\n【竞争词】A) bond 单数形态不匹配复数主语要求\nbond 是单数，need to 与 are 一致要求复数；且\"积极可能的纽带\"语义略生硬，不如\noutcomes 概括整段所述\"可能性\"。\nD) hierarchy 语义贬义偏冷且单数\nhierarchy 表\"等级\"，多含层级、约束意味，与 positive 搭配不自然；且单数与 are 不一致。\nK) patterns 语义不指向\"结果\"\npatterns 表\"模式\"，与\"成年子女同住带来的好处\"这一结果性陈述语义错位。\nN) stereotypes 感情色彩与 positive 冲突\nstereotypes 是贬义\"刻板印象\"，与 positive possible 之间存在感情色彩冲突，逻辑上也讲不\n通\"积极的刻板印象\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 positive possibl"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) bond\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 strong 后缺单数可数名词\n【依据】a strong bond 是\"牢固关系\"的固定搭配\na strong __(33)__ doesn't simply come with time 与上一段 the \"parent-child\nrelationship may indeed strengthen and mature\" 一脉相承——心理学家说的 \"a strong\nbond\"（一段牢固的纽带/亲密关系）正是对前文 parent-child relationship 的换说法。\n【竞争词】D) hierarchy 语义反向\nhierarchy 表\"等级\"，与本段强调的\"平等而紧密的亲子关系\"语义相反；上一段刚说\"the power\ndynamic … no longer defined by giver and taker\"，恰好否定等级关系。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 strong 后缺单数可数名词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) magically\n副词\n【词性槽】实义动词 connect 前缺状语\n【依据】与 mere passage of time 配合，表达\"靠时间神奇地解决\"的反讽语气\nThe many changing factors of the relationship need to be acknowledged, rather\nthan hoping that the mere passage of time will __(34)__ connect parents to their\nadult children 全句在批驳一种幻想：以为\"时间一过\"就能自动让父母与成年子女连结起来\n——magically 正好刻画这种\"指望奇迹般发生\"的天真，并与上句 \"a strong bond doesn't\nsimply come with time\" 呼应。\n【竞争词】O) undoubtedly 肯定语气与作者立场相悖\nundoubtedly 表\"毫无疑问地\"，等于替\"时间自动让关系变近\"背书；可作者整段都在反对这种想\n法，方向完全相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·实义动词 connect 前缺状语"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bond", "B) contemplated", "C) dawned", "D) hierarchy", "E) insight", "F) legislative", "G) leverage", "H) logistical", "I) magically", "J) outcomes", "K) patterns", "L) rarely", "M) saturated", "N) stereotypes", "O) undoubtedly"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) patterns\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 into + 形容词 the teen years 限定 → 复数名词宾语\n【依据】fall back into patterns 是\"重新陷入旧有相处模式\"的固定表达\nIt's important to acknowledge that the relationship parameters have changed to\navoid falling back into __(35)__ from the teen years：作者主张要承认\"关系参数\"已变，\n以免退回少年时期那种父母-青春期子女的旧相处套路；patterns（行为模式）与\n\"parameters have changed\" 精确对仗——参数变了，就别再走旧 patterns。\n【竞争词】D) hierarchy 范围过窄、单数形态偏差\nhierarchy 指等级，仅是\"少年期父母权威\"这一种侧面，无法概括 \"the teen years\" 整个相处\n面；且 hierarchy 多用单数，不太与 from the teen years（暗示多种旧习）的复数意味契合。\nN) stereotypes 对象不对（刻板印象指向他人，而非两人相处模式）\nstereotypes 是社会对某群体的\"刻板印象\"，常带歧视含义；本句讲的是\"亲子之间过去的互动方\n式\"，patterns 更准确，stereotypes 与具体相处情境错位。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— K) patterns 与 N) stereotypes 同为复数名词且都\"看着\"和\n成长话题沾边。考生若仅看到 the teen years 就联想\"刻板印象\"，容易误选 stereotypes；\n但本句讲两人相处旧模式，不讲社会偏见。防坑：fall back into 后面通常接\npatterns/habits/routines，而非 stereotypes。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 into + 形容词 the te"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "An overwhelming majority of family physicians are willing to use telemedicine if they are duly paid.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】家庭医生压倒性多数愿用条件\n【定位】In a poll of 1500 family physicians, only 15% had used it in their practices—but 90% said\nthey would if it were appropriately reimbursed.\n在一项对 1500 名家庭医生的调查中，只有 15% 在自己的诊疗中用过远程医疗——但 90%\n表示，只要能得到合理补偿，他们就愿意使用。\n【改写】An overwhelming majority of family physicians ↔ 90% said they would\n比例数字=压倒性多数\nif they are duly paid ↔ if it were appropriately reimbursed(补偿)\nduly paid=appropriately reimbursed\n【辨邻】 K K 段确实大量谈论是否为远程医疗付费，但主语是保险公司与 Medicare，不是 family\nphysicians 的意愿，所以只是话题相邻。\ninsurers have been far less willing to pay for telemedicine when doctors use phone, email or video\nto consult with existing patients about continuing issues", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Many employers are eager to provide telemedicine service as a benefit to their employees because of its convenience.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】雇主把远程医疗当员工福利\n【定位】Many health plans and employers have rushed to offer the services and promote them as a\nconvenient way for plan members to get medical care without leaving home or work.\n许多健康计划和雇主已经抢着提供这项服务，并将其作为一种便利方式推广，让参保员工无\n需离家或离开工作岗位就能就医。\n【改写】Many employers are eager to provide telemedicine service as a benefit to their employees ↔\nNearly three-quarters of large employers will offer virtual doctor visits as a benefit to\nemployees this year\nnear three-quarters=many+词组对应\nbecause of its convenience ↔\npromote them as a convenient way for plan members to get medical care without leaving home\nor work\nconvenience=convenient way\n【辨邻】 G G 段也提便利（on-demand, 24/7），但主语是 consumers and clinicians，没有出\n现『雇主把它作为员工福利』这一陈述。\nThe fastest-growing services in telemedicine connect consumers with clinicians they've never met\nfor a phone, video or email visit—on-demand, 24/7", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Different states have markedly different regulations for telemedicine.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】各州监管差异\n【定位】Rules defining and regulating telemedicine differ widely from state to state.\n界定与监管远程医疗的规则在各州之间差异极大。\n【改写】Different states have markedly different regulations for telemedicine ↔\nRules defining and regulating telemedicine differ widely from state to state\n句式重构+程度词对译\n【辨邻】 N N 段虽然也提『50 套规则各异』，但目的是引出 is hampering its growth，落点\n在因果与影响，不是 Q38 想要的纯差异陈述；它与第 45 题（强调 hinders）一一\n对应。\nhaving 50 different sets of rules, licensing fees and even definitions of \"medical practice\" makes\nless sense in the era of telemedicine and is hampering its growth\nO O 谈 17 州互认、200 多项法案，是面向解决方案的描述，不是题干所要的『差异存\n在』陈述。\nA lot of states are still trying to define telemedicine", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "With telemedicine, patients in regions short of professional medical service are able to receive better medical care.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】专业医疗资源稀缺地区受益\n【定位】Telemedicine also allows for better care in places where medical expertise is hard to come by.\n远程医疗还能让那些医疗专业资源稀缺的地方获得更好的照护。\n【改写】patients in regions short of professional medical service ↔\nin places where medical expertise is hard to come by\nregions=places+稀缺改写\nable to receive better medical care ↔ allows for better care\n主动接受=被允许获得", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Unlike employers and health plans, insurers have been rather reluctant to pay for some telemedicine services.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】保险公司与雇主/健康计划态度对比\n【定位】While employers and health plans have been eager to cover virtual urgent-care visits, insurers\nhave been far less willing to pay for telemedicine when doctors use phone, email or video to\nconsult with existing patients about continuing issues.\n尽管雇主和健康计划积极覆盖虚拟急诊就诊，但当医生通过电话、邮件或视频就持续性问题\n为现有患者提供咨询时，保险公司就远没那么乐意付费了。\n【改写】Unlike employers and health plans ↔\nWhile employers and health plans have been eager to cover virtual urgent-care visits\nUnlike=While 让步对照\ninsurers have been rather reluctant to pay for some telemedicine services ↔\ninsurers have been far less willing to pay for telemedicine\nreluctant=far less willing\n【辨邻】 H H 段也讲 employers/health plans 推广远程医疗，但没有同时把 insurers 不愿付费\n的反面态度也陈述出来；缺少对照中『B』那一边，不能当源头段。\nMany health plans and employers have rushed to offer the services and promote them as a\nconvenient way for plan members to get medical care without leaving home or work", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Some supporters of telemedicine hope states will accept each other's medical practice licenses as valid.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】支持者希望各州互认执照\n【定位】While welcoming the move, some telemedicine advocates would prefer states to\nautomatically honor one another's licenses, as they do with drivers' licenses.\n尽管欢迎这一举措，一些远程医疗倡导者更希望各州像处理驾照那样，自动互认彼此颁发的\n医疗执照。\n【改写】Some supporters of telemedicine ↔ some telemedicine advocates\nsupporters=advocates\nhope states will accept each other's medical practice licenses as valid ↔\nwould prefer states to automatically honor one another's licenses, as they do with drivers'\nlicenses\nhope=prefer+accept...as valid=honor\n【辨邻】 N N 段提到 licensing fees 与 doctors must have a valid license in the state where\nthe patient is located，落点是问题与限制，没有『支持者希望互认』的愿望陈述。\nCurrently, doctors must have a valid license in the state where the patient is located to provide\nmedical care", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The fastest growing area for telemedicine services is for lesser health problems.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】增长最快的领域=小毛病\n【定位】The fastest-growing services in telemedicine connect consumers with clinicians they've never\nmet for a phone, video or email visit—on-demand, 24/7. Typically, these are for\nnonemergency issues such as colds, flu, ear-aches and skin rashes, and they cost around $45.\n远程医疗中增长最快的服务，是把消费者与从未谋面的临床医师通过电话、视频或邮件随时\n（每周 7 天每天 24 小时）连接起来；这些服务通常用于感冒、流感、耳痛、皮疹等非急诊\n问题，每次大约花费 45 美元。\n【改写】The fastest growing area for telemedicine services ↔\nThe fastest-growing services in telemedicine\n最高级近乎逐字一致\nlesser health problems ↔ nonemergency issues such as colds, flu, ear-aches and skin rashes\n上位概括=非急诊+具体举例\n【辨邻】 H H 段也讲 1.2 million such virtual doctor visits this year, up 20% from last year，\n但谈的是『总量增长百分比』，不是题干所要的『增长最快的领域=小毛病』这一\n陈述。\nWeb companies such as Teladoc and American Well are expected to host some 1.2 million such\nvirtual doctor visits this year, up 20% from last year", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "As telemedicine spreads quickly, some of its opponents doubt whether its service quality can be guaranteed.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】反对者质疑服务质量\n【定位】Some critics also question whether the quality of care is keeping up with the rapid expansion\nof telemedicine.\n一些批评者也质疑医疗服务的质量能否跟上远程医疗快速扩张的步伐。\n【改写】As telemedicine spreads quickly ↔ the rapid expansion of telemedicine\nspreads quickly=rapid expansion\nsome of its opponents doubt whether its service quality can be guaranteed ↔\nSome critics also question whether the quality of care is keeping up with the rapid expansion\nof telemedicine\nopponents=critics+doubt=question\n【辨邻】 I I 段是 F 段质量担忧的延伸——通过 16 个网站的实验来支撑批评；它讲的是具体研究\n而非题干所要的总括陈述。\ncritics worry that such services may be sacrificing quality for convenience", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The results obtained by researchers who pretended to be patients seeking help from telemedicine providers are disturbing.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】研究者假扮患者的结果令人不安\n【定位】In a recent study, researchers posing as patients with skin problems sought help from 16\ntelemedicine sites—with unsettling results.\n在最近一项研究中，研究者假扮皮肤病患者向 16 家远程医疗网站求助——结果令人不安。\n【改写】researchers who pretended to be patients seeking help from telemedicine providers ↔\nresearchers posing as patients with skin problems sought help from 16 telemedicine sites\npretend to be=pose as\nare disturbing ↔ unsettling results\ndisturbing=unsettling", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some people argue that the fact that different states have different regulations concerning medical services hinders the development of telemedicine.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】州际规则差异阻碍发展\n【定位】But some industry members contend that having 50 different sets of rules, licensing fees and\neven definitions of \"medical practice\" makes less sense in the era of telemedicine and is\nhampering its growth.\n但一些业内人士认为，在远程医疗时代有 50 套不同的规则、执照收费乃至『医疗实践』的\n定义都越来越说不通，并且正在妨碍其发展。\n【改写】different states have different regulations concerning medical services ↔\nhaving 50 different sets of rules, licensing fees and even definitions of \"medical practice\"\n差异事实双向对齐\nhinders the development of telemedicine ↔ is hampering its growth\nhinder=hamper+development=growth\n【辨邻】 E E 段确实陈述各州规则差异，但没有把差异与『阻碍远程医疗发展』连成因果；题干带\nhinders 这一因果动词，应选有因果链的 N。\nRules defining and regulating telemedicine differ widely from state to state", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about Glamour magazine readers?", "options": ["A) They are intrigued by the exotic romance in Danielle Steel's novels.", "B) They are amazed by the number of books written by Danielle Steel.", "C) They are deeply impressed by Danielle Steel's daily work schedule.", "D) They are highly motivated by Danielle Steel's unusual productivity."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about Glamour magazine readers?问的是关于一\n个具体对象的客观信息，没有infer/imply等推断信号词。\n【拆句】题干结构平铺直叙，主句What do we learn from the passage about Glamour\nmagazine readers?是一个标准的wh-疑问句，from the passage是状语、about Glamour\nmagazine readers是learn的逻辑宾语，无陷阱。\n【定位】But a passing reference in a recent profile by Glamour magazine to her 20-hour workdays\nprompted an outpouring of admiration.\n但《Glamour》杂志近期一篇侧写中顺带提到她每天工作20小时，激起了一阵由衷的赞叹。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nexotic romance in Danielle Steel's novels这层\"异国浪漫\"的内容在第1段乃至全文都未\n出现，纯属凭借\"romance novelist\"的身份想当然添加。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文第1段\"读者赞叹\"的触发点是her 20-hour workdays，而非the number of books\nwritten by Danielle Steel。把前半句的179本书当成了赞叹的来源。\nC) ✓ 正确\nher 20-hour workdays ↔ Danielle Steel's daily work schedule\nprompted an outpouring of admiration ↔ deeply impressed\n预判的两要素\"每日作息\"与\"印象深刻\"，只有They are deeply impressed by Danielle\nSteel's daily work schedule.同时覆盖。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文写的是读者被prompted an outpouring of admiration——\"激起赞叹\"，是被动情\n感反应，并未说读者\"被激励去行动\"；highly motivated把\"赞叹\"偷换成了\"高度受激\n励\"，且把对象\"daily schedule\"偷换成了\"unusual productivity\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did the business news website Quartz say about Danielle Steel?", "options": ["A) She could serve as an example of industriousness.", "B) She proved we could liberate ourselves from sleep.", "C) She could be an inspiration to novelists all over the world.", "D) She showed we could get all our work done without sleep."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What did the business news website Quartz say about Danielle Steel?问的是某主体说\n过的具体观点，属于带主语的言论复述题。\n【拆句】What did the business news website Quartz say about Danielle Steel?中the business\nnews website是Quartz的同位语，提示考生Quartz是个网站，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】The business news website Quartz held Steel up as an inspiration, writing that if only we all\nfollowed her \"actually extremely liberating\" example of industrious sleeplessness, we would\nbe quick to see results.\n商业新闻网站Quartz把Steel树为榜样，写道：要是我们都效仿她那\"其实极具解放感\"的\"勤\n奋不眠\"，就能很快见到成效。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nheld Steel up as an inspiration ↔ serve as an example of industriousness\nA把held up as an inspiration改写成serve as an example，并把industrious改写为\nindustriousness，预判两要素都落到位。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说if only we all followed her…example…we would be quick to see results\n——\"效仿则见效\"，并未断言\"我们可以摆脱睡眠\"；we could liberate ourselves from\nsleep把liberating这个形容词推过了头。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文if only we all followed her里的we all指所有人，novelists all over the world把\n对象悄悄缩小到\"全世界小说家\"，与原文范围不一致。\nD) 过度推断\n原文是we would be quick to see results——\"见效快\"，并未承诺\"无眠也能把工作全\n做完\"；get all our work done without sleep中all与without sleep都是被推过头的绝\n对化表述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do sceptics think of Danielle Steel's work schedule claims?", "options": ["A) They are questionable.", "B) They are alterable.", "C) They are irresistible.", "D) They are verifiable."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do sceptics think of Danielle Steel's work schedule claims?问某群体对某事的看\n法，是观点复述题，仍属细节题范畴。\n【拆句】What do sceptics think of Danielle Steel's work schedule claims?中of引出thinking的\n对象，work schedule claims是名词组：work schedule是名词修饰claims，意为\"关于作\n息时间表的说法\"。\n【定位】Her output may be undeniable, but sceptics have suggested that she is guilty of erasing the\nrole of ghost writers(代笔人) at worst, gross exaggeration at best.\n她的产量诚然毋庸置疑，但怀疑者认为她最严重的过错是抹去了代笔人的功劳，最轻也是严\n重夸大。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ngross exaggeration at best ↔ questionable\nshe is guilty of erasing the role of ghost writers ↔ questionable\n四个选项只有questionable能同时容纳\"夸大\"与\"隐瞒代笔\"两端的负面极性。\nB) 无中生有\nalterable意为\"可更改的\"，原文怀疑者只判定Steel说法不真，从未提及作息表是\n否\"可改\"。\nC) 与原文相反\nirresistible意为\"不可抗拒的、极具诱惑力的\"，是正面色彩词，与怀疑者的负面态度完全\n相反。\nD) 与原文相反\nverifiable意为\"可证实的\"，意味着\"经得起验证\"——这与怀疑者认为Steel\"夸大/隐\n瞒\"恰好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does Maryanne Taylor think of self-imposed sleeplessness?", "options": ["A) It may turn out to be key to a successful career.", "B) It may be practiced only by certain tech heads.", "C) It may symbolise one's importance and success.", "D) It may well serve as a measure of self-discipline."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does Maryanne Taylor think of self-imposed sleeplessness?问具名人物对某概念\n的看法，是观点复述题。\n【拆句】What does Maryanne Taylor think of self-imposed sleeplessness?中self-imposed是过\n去分词作前置定语，意为\"自我强加的\"，修饰sleeplessness。\n【定位】Self-imposed sleeplessness has \"become a bit of a status symbol\", says Taylor, a misguided\nmeasure to prove how powerful and productive you are.\nTaylor说，自我强加的失眠\"已经变成了一种身份象征\"——一种被误用来证明自己强大、高\n效的方式。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nTaylor明确说自我强加失眠是a misguided measure——\"被误用的方式\"，他绝不会认\n为这是\"成功职业的关键\"；况且key to a successful career这层观点的源头是第6段\n末\"科技大佬\"那一句的他人观点，与Taylor的判断不同。\nB) 以偏概全\n原文tech heads只是举例之一Margaret Thatcher was also said to get by on four\nhours a night, while the 130-hour workweeks endured by tech heads have been\nheld up as key to their success，并未说\"只被某些科技大佬实践\"；practiced only by\ncertain tech heads中的only是绝对化偷换。\nC) ✓ 正确\na status symbol ↔ symbolise one's importance and success\nto prove how powerful and productive you are ↔ one's importance and success\nC同时把status symbol改成symbolise，把powerful/productive改成\nimportance/success，预判两要素都落到位。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文明确说自我强加失眠是a misguided measure——\"被误用的方式\"，而serve as a\nmeasure of self-discipline把它正面解读为\"自律的标尺\"，态度方向完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "How does Dr. Sophie Bostock look at the 20-hour daily work schedule?", "options": ["A) One should not adopt it without consulting a sleep expert.", "B) The general public should not be encouraged to follow it.", "C) One must be duly self-disciplined to adhere to it.", "D) The majority must adjust their body clock for it."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】How does Dr. Sophie Bostock look at the 20-hour daily work schedule?问具名人物如\n何看待某事，仍是观点复述题。\n【拆句】How does Dr. Sophie Bostock look at the 20-hour daily work schedule?中look at\n取\"看待\"义而非\"看\"义，是熟词僻义；20-hour是名词作前置修饰daily work schedule的复\n合定语。\n【定位】Even if Steel does happen to be among that tiny minority, says Bostock, it's \"pretty\nirresponsible\" to suggest that 20-hour days are simply a question of discipline for the rest\nof us.\nBostock说，即便Steel恰巧属于那极少数人，向我们其他人暗示\"每天20小时只是个自律问\n题\"也是相当不负责任的。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nwithout consulting a sleep expert这一条件原文从未出现，Bostock只说\"对大众宣扬\n这是纪律问题不负责任\"，并未给出\"先咨询专家再采用\"的建议。\nB) ✓ 正确\nit's \"pretty irresponsible\" to suggest that 20-hour days are simply a question of discipline for the\nrest of us\n↔ The general public should not be encouraged to follow it.\nthe rest of us ↔ The general public\nB精确捕捉了\"对象=普通大众\"与\"立场=不该鼓励\"两端，预判与之吻合。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文it's \"pretty irresponsible\" to suggest that 20-hour days are simply a question\nof discipline——Bostock明确反对把它简化为纪律问题；duly self-disciplined to\nadhere to it反而把\"自律\"当作前提，与Bostock立场相反。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只提及Steel可能born a \"short sleeper\" with an unusual body clock——这是\"天\n生\"的极少数情况，并非建议大众去\"调整生物钟\"；adjust their body clock凭空生造了\n一种行动建议。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn from the conclusion of the author's review study?", "options": ["A) More resources should be tapped for feeding the world's population.", "B) Organic farming may be exploited to solve the global food problem.", "C) The long-term prospects of organic farming are yet to be explored.", "D) Organic farming is at least as promising as conventional farming."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from the conclusion of the author's review study? 是 What 提\n问且指向作者明确表述过的结论，没有 infer/imply 等推断信号。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn from the conclusion of the author's review study? 是疑问句，\n主干为 What do we learn，from the conclusion of the author's review study 是介词短\n语作状语，说明\"从哪里得知\"。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Organic agriculture is a relatively untapped resource for feeding the Earth's population,\nespecially in the face of climate change and other global challenges. That's the conclusion I\nreached in reviewing 40 years of science comparing the long-term prospects of organic and\nconventional farming.\n有机农业是一项相对尚未被开发利用的资源，可用于养活地球人口，尤其是在气候变化与\n其他全球挑战面前。这是我回顾对比有机与常规农业长期前景的 40 年科学研究后得出的\n结论。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文说\"有机农业是一种尚未被开发的资源\"，选项却把主语换成了 More resources——\n主体从\"有机农业本身是资源\"被偷换成了\"应开发更多资源\"，丢失了\"有机\"这一核心\n限定。\nB) ✓ 正确\nOrganic agriculture is a relatively untapped resource for feeding the Earth's population ↔\nOrganic farming may be exploited to solve the global food problem\nespecially in the face of climate change and other global challenges ↔\nthe global food problem\n预判要求表达\"有机农业可被开发利用来应对全球粮食问题\"，Organic farming may\nbe exploited to solve the global food problem 与之一一对应，是定位句的标准同义\n改写。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 That's the conclusion I reached 表明作者已经得出结论；选项 are yet to be\nexplored 却说\"长远前景仍有待探索\"，与原文完全相反。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 Organic farming is at least as promising as conventional farming 是\"二者前\n景至少同等光明\"的并列断言，原文结论句只说有机是\"未被开发的资源\"、未把二者做\"at\nleast as...as\"的并列评估。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the critics' argument against organic farming?", "options": ["A) It cannot meet the need for food.", "B) It cannot increase farm yields.", "C) It is not really practical.", "D) It is not that productive."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the critics' argument against organic farming? 直接问\"具体论点是什么\"，\n对象明确、问法直接。\n【拆句】题干 What is the critics' argument against organic farming? 主干为 What is the\nargument，critics' 是所有格定语，against organic farming 是介词短语作定语修饰\nargument，整体说\"批评者针对有机农业的论点\"。\n【定位】Critics have long argued that organic agriculture is inefficient, requiring more land to yield\nthe same amount of food. It's true that organic farming produces lower yields, averaging 10 to\n20 percent less than conventional.\n批评者长期以来一直主张有机农业效率低下，需要更多土地才能产出同等数量的食物。事实\n上，有机农业的产量确实较低，平均比常规农业少 10% 到 20%。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文只说产量低、效率差，未说有机农业\"不能满足粮食需求\"；选项 It cannot meet\nthe need for food 把\"低产\"向前推了一步变成\"喂不饱人\"，超出原文。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文是\"目前产量低\"的状态描述，选项 It cannot increase farm yields 却说\"无法提高\n产量\"——后者是动态能力评价，原文没有。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文批评的是\"低效、产量低\"，选项 It is not really practical 笼统说\"不实际\"——\npractical 含\"可行/实用/落地\"等多义，比原文具体所指（产出效率）宽得多，属泛化。\nD) ✓ 正确\norganic agriculture is inefficient ↔ It is not that productive\nrequiring more land to yield the same amount of food ↔ not that productive\n预判要求表达\"产量/生产效率不高\"，It is not that productive 正是\"低产/低效\"的同义\n改写，与定位句中 inefficient + lower yields 严丝合缝。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author think should be taken into account in arguing about organic farming?", "options": ["A) Growth in world population.", "B) Deterioration in soil fertility.", "C) Inequality in food distribution.", "D) Advance in farming technology."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author think should be taken into account in arguing about\norganic farming? 问\"作者认为应当考虑什么\"，所问内容是文中作者明确点出过的事实\n因素。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author think should be taken into account in arguing about\norganic farming? 含 What...should be taken into account 的被动结构，主语 What 是宾\n语前置，in arguing about organic farming 是介词短语作状语；理解时把它还原成\"作者\n认为在讨论有机农业时应当考虑什么\"。\n【定位】Sometimes excluded from these arguments is the fact that we already produce enough\nfood to more than feed the world's 7.4 billion people but do not provide adequate access to\nall individuals.\n这些争论中有时被遗漏的一个事实是：我们已经生产出了足以养活全球 74 亿人有余的食\n物，但并未给所有人提供充分的获取渠道。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文只给出当前人口数 7.4 billion 作为\"已够吃\"的参照，并未谈\"人口增长\"；选项\nGrowth in world population 把静态数据偷换为动态趋势。\nB) 答非所问\n选项 Deterioration in soil fertility 谈\"土壤肥力恶化\"，这是文章后文 P7 涉及的土壤话\n题，但不是 P3 \"争论中被遗漏的事实\"，与本题无关。\nC) ✓ 正确\ndo not provide adequate access to all individuals ↔ Inequality in food distribution\nwe already produce enough food to more than feed the world's 7.4 billion people ↔\nInequality in food distribution\n预判要求表达\"粮食分配不均/获取不公\"，Inequality in food distribution 正是其名词\n化形式，与定位句严丝合缝。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 Advance in farming technology 谈\"农业技术进步\"，全段 P3 未出现该话题，全\n文亦无对应；属凭空生造。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does science tell us about conventional farming?", "options": ["A) It will not be able to meet global food demand.", "B) It is not conducive to sustainable development.", "C) It will eventually give away to organic farming.", "D) It is going mainstream throughout the world."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does science tell us about conventional farming? 是 What 提问，问\"科学告\n诉我们\"的明示内容，属直陈式提问。\n【拆句】题干 What does science tell us about conventional farming? 主干为 What does\nscience tell us，about conventional farming 是介词短语作状语，限定话题范围。结构平\n铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】What science does tell us is that mainstream conventional farming systems have\nprovided growing supplies of food and other products but often at the expense of other\nsustainability goals.\n科学确实告诉我们的是：主流的常规农业体系虽提供了不断增长的食物及其他产品供给，却\n往往以牺牲其他可持续目标为代价。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文说常规农业\"现在已在牺牲可持续目标\"，选项 It will not be able to meet global\nfood demand 却预言\"未来无法满足全球粮食需求\"，未来式预言+绝对否定均超出\n原文。\nB) ✓ 正确\nat the expense of other sustainability goals ↔ It is not conducive to sustainable development\nbut often at the expense of other sustainability goals ↔\nIt is not conducive to sustainable development\n预判要求表达\"常规农业不利于可持续发展\"，It is not conducive to sustainable\ndevelopment 是这一判断的标准同义改写，与定位句转折后的部分一一对应。\nC) 无中生有\n选项 It will eventually give away to organic farming 说常规农业\"终将让位于有机农\n业\"，文中无此预测；作者立场是\"应同时考虑有机潜力\"，并未断言谁取代谁。\nD) 答非所问\n原文用 mainstream 是为引出对常规农业的批评，选项 It is going mainstream\nthroughout the world 却把它处理为\"正在全球范围流行\"，并丢掉了 but 转折后的批评\n内核——属答非所问。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "Why does the author think higher prices of organic farm produce are justifiable?", "options": ["A) They give farmers going organic a big competitive edge.", "B) They motivate farmers to upgrade farming technology.", "C) Organic farming costs more than conventional farming.", "D) Organic farming does long-term good to the ecosystem."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why does the author think higher prices of organic farm produce are\njustifiable? 是 Why 提问且作者已明确写出理由，属直接细节题，不需要跨段推断。\n【拆句】题干 Why does the author think higher prices of organic farm produce are\njustifiable? 是 Why 引导的疑问句，含一个嵌套宾语从句 higher prices...are justifiable；\n理解时还原为\"作者为什么认为...是合理的\"。\n【定位】Higher prices, called price premiums, can be justified as a way to compensate farmers for\nproviding ecosystem services and avoiding environmental damage or external costs.\n更高的价格——称为\"价格溢价\"——可以被合理化解释为一种对农民的补偿，是因为他们提\n供了生态系统服务、避免了环境损害或外部成本。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是高价对生态贡献的补偿合理性，选项 They give farmers going organic a\nbig competitive edge 把它换成\"给做有机的农民带来巨大竞争优势\"——竞争话语不是\n文中评价高价的角度，属偷换。\nB) 无中生有\n选项 They motivate farmers to upgrade farming technology 谈\"高价激励农民升\n级技术\"，文中既未把 motivate 与 higher prices 挂钩，也未提\"升级技术\"，属凭空\n生造。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文给出的合理化依据是\"补偿生态服务+避免环境损害\"，选项 Organic farming costs\nmore than conventional farming 改为\"有机生产成本比常规高\"——文中并未把高价归\n因于生产成本对比，属偷换。\nD) ✓ 正确\nproviding ecosystem services and avoiding environmental damage or external costs ↔\nOrganic farming does long-term good to the ecosystem\ncan be justified as a way to compensate farmers ↔\nOrganic farming does long-term good to the ecosystem\n预判要求表达\"有机农业长期上对生态有益\"，Organic farming does long-term good\nto the ecosystem 把原文中\"提供生态服务+避免环境损害\"合并为\"做长期好事\"，与定\n位句因果同向。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2020-09-1"});
